# Tutorial 12 — Best Practices
# A checklist of habits that keep your VNS project clean, readable, and scalable.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_12_best_practices
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
{{BG_START}}[inc tutorial_count]
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: You've learned the commands. Now let's talk about the habits that separate a hobby project from a polished game.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: These aren't rules — they're lessons from projects that grew big enough to hurt.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: What would you like to review?
> File and folder hygiene -> section_files
> Naming conventions -> section_naming
> Dialogue writing tips -> section_dialogue
> Testing and playtesting -> section_testing
> Common mistakes -> section_mistakes
> The full checklist -> reference_sheet
> Quiz yourself -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── File and Folder Hygiene ─────────────────────────────────────

@label section_files
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Your project structure is the first thing a collaborator sees. Make it welcoming.
narrator: {b}scripts/{/b} — all VNS files, organized by chapter or arc.
narrator: {b}assets/characters/{/b} — character sprites, one folder per character.
narrator: {b}assets/backgrounds/{/b} — scene backgrounds.
narrator: {b}assets/audio/bgm/{/b} — music files.
narrator: {b}assets/audio/sfx/{/b} — sound effects.
narrator: {b}config/{/b} — menus, layouts, styles, registry files.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The exact structure depends on your project, but the principle is universal: {i}group by type, name by purpose{/i}.
Lavender: Don't dump everything in one folder. Don't use cryptic folder names. Don't mix audio with scripts.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: If someone new joins the team, they should be able to find any file in under 30 seconds.
> Next: Naming -> section_naming
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Naming Conventions ──────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_naming
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Good names prevent bugs. Bad names cause them.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: For {b}files{/b}:
narrator: {b}Good:{/b} ch1_rooftop_scene.vns, ch2_library_confrontation.vns
narrator: {b}Bad:{/b} scene3.vns, new_file_FINAL_v2.vns
Lavender: For {b}labels{/b}:
narrator: {b}Good:{/b} rooftop_choice, after_confrontation, ending_brave
narrator: {b}Bad:{/b} label1, temp, here
Lavender: For {b}variables{/b}:
narrator: {b}Good:{/b} courage, met_librarian, ch1_route_active
narrator: {b}Bad:{/b} x, flag, val
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Use snake_case everywhere. Prefix chapter-specific variables. Be descriptive even when it feels verbose.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: The name should answer "what is this?" without needing to read the surrounding code.
> Next: Dialogue -> section_dialogue
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Dialogue Writing Tips ───────────────────────────────────────

@label section_dialogue
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Technical skill gets the engine running. Writing skill makes the player care. A few things to keep in mind:
narrator: {b}Keep lines short.{/b} Two sentences per dialogue line, max. Players read fast.
narrator: {b}Read it aloud.{/b} If it sounds awkward spoken, it reads awkward too.
narrator: {b}Give characters voice.{/b} Each character should sound different — vocabulary, sentence length, attitude.
narrator: {b}Use narration for atmosphere.{/b} Don't make characters describe what the player can see.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: And the golden rule of VN dialogue:
narrator: {b}Every line should either advance the plot, reveal character, or create mood.{/b}
narrator: If it doesn't do any of those, cut it.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Your player's time is precious. Respect it, and they'll respect your story.
> Next: Testing -> section_testing
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Testing and Playtesting ────────────────────────────────────

@label section_testing
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: You will ship bugs. The question is how many. Here's how to minimize them:
narrator: {b}Play every branch.{/b} Don't just test the happy path. Click every choice.
narrator: {b}Check your variables.{/b} After each scene, verify counters and flags are what you expect.
narrator: {b}Test saves and loads.{/b} Save mid-scene, reload, and make sure nothing breaks.
narrator: {b}Watch for dead ends.{/b} Every label should either lead somewhere or end the scene intentionally.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Get someone else to playtest. You've read your script a hundred times — you'll miss things. Fresh eyes catch what you can't.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: And test on the target resolution. UI elements that look fine at 1920x1080 might overlap at 1280x720.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Budget time for testing. It's not optional — it's where quality lives.
> Next: Common mistakes -> section_mistakes
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Common Mistakes ─────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_mistakes
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The mistakes I see most often:
narrator: {b}Missing [endif].{/b} Every [if] needs one. The error is silent but the behavior is wrong.
narrator: {b}Wrong scenario ID in [goto].{/b} Typos in scenario names cause hard-to-debug navigation failures.
narrator: {b}Forgetting [return].{/b} A [call] without a matching [return] runs off the end of the label.
narrator: {b}Overusing variables.{/b} If you have 100 flags and can't remember what half of them do, simplify.
narrator: {b}Walls of text.{/b} If a single dialogue line fills the entire text box, split it.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: And the sneaky one:
narrator: {b}Untested branches.{/b} You wrote it, but did you play it? There's always one path nobody clicked.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Most of these are caught by simply playing your own game start to finish. Do it before anyone else does.
> The full checklist -> reference_sheet
> Quiz yourself -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Reference Checklist ─────────────────────────────────────────

@label reference_sheet
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The best practices checklist:
narrator: {b}Structure:{/b} One scenario per file. Descriptive file names. Organized folders.
narrator: {b}Naming:{/b} snake_case for labels and variables. Chapter prefixes for scoped state.
narrator: {b}Dialogue:{/b} Short lines. Distinct character voices. Cut anything that doesn't earn its place.
narrator: {b}Flow:{/b} Use [call]/[return] for reusable blocks. Merge branches when possible.
narrator: {b}Variables:{/b} Descriptive names. Minimal count. Document what each one tracks.
narrator: {b}Testing:{/b} Play every branch. Test saves. Check variable state. Get fresh eyes.
narrator: {b}Assets:{/b} Organized by type. Consistent naming. No orphan files.
narrator: {b}Conditions:{/b} Every [if] has [endif]. Keep blocks short. Avoid deep nesting.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Print this, pin it to your wall, and check it before every release. Seriously.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice Quiz ───────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Quick quiz — no pressure. Just checking your instincts.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Question 1: You have a greeting that three different scenes use. What's the best approach?
> Copy-paste it into each scene -> practice_q1_copy
> Use [call] to a shared greeting label -> practice_q1_call
> Write three different greetings -> practice_q1_three

@label practice_q1_copy
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: That works, but now you have three copies to maintain. Change one, forget the others — instant inconsistency.
[set practice_score 0]
[jump practice_q2]

@label practice_q1_call
[inc practice_score]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Exactly. One source of truth, called from wherever you need it. Change it once, it updates everywhere.
[jump practice_q2]

@label practice_q1_three
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: That can work for character variety, but if they're supposed to be the same greeting, you're tripling your maintenance.
[set practice_score 0]
[jump practice_q2]

@label practice_q2
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Question 2: A variable is named "x" and tracks how many times the player helped NPCs. What should you rename it to?
> npc_help_count -> practice_q2_good
> flag1 -> practice_q2_bad
> x is fine -> practice_q2_x

@label practice_q2_good
[inc practice_score]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Perfect. Descriptive, clear, and you'll know what it means six months from now.
[jump practice_result]

@label practice_q2_bad
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: "flag1" is barely better than "x". What does it flag? When? Why?
[jump practice_result]

@label practice_q2_x
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: It works... until you have 30 variables and can't remember what any of them do. Trust me.
[jump practice_result]

@label practice_result
[show lavender center talking]
[if practice_score >= 2]
Lavender: 2 out of 2. You've got the right instincts — clean code, clear names, reusable structure.
[elif practice_score == 1]
Lavender: 1 out of 2. You're on the right track. The habits build with practice.
[else]
Lavender: 0 out of 2. No worries — knowing the principles is what matters. You can always refactor.
[endif]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: That's all 12 tutorials. You've got every tool you need. Now go build something worth playing.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_2]
